          %h2#introduction Introduction
          %p
            For
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \gt 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi(x)
            denote the number of primes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \leq x
            (in these notes,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            is always used to denote prime numbers). The celebrated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prime+number+theorem"} Prime Number Theorem
            is the asymptotic statement
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∼
                  %mfrac
                    %mi x
                    %mrow
                      %mi log
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi(x) \sim \frac{x}{\log(x)}
          %p
            where we recall that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∼
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x) \sim g(x)
            means
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mi lim
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo →
                        %mn ∞
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underset{x \to \infty}{\lim} \frac{f(x)}{g(x)} = 1
          %p
            This was first proven in 1896 independently by Hadamard and de la Vallée-Poussin, first by proving a result on the zeroes of the Riemann zeta function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ζ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \zeta(s)
            (that none occur on the line
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Re
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Re(s) = 1
          %p
            Over the decades this analysis was whittled down more and more until it reached a fairly definitive form, in the form of a succinct “Tauberian theorem” due to
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#new"} D.J. Newman (1980)
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#zag1"} Don Zagier (1997)
          %p
            As in his famous
            %a{:href => "#zag2"} one-sentence proof
            that primes of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 4
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 4n+1
            are sums of two squares, Zagier seemed to be aiming explicitly for maximal compression while still keeping the argument (arguably) self-contained. It’s an impressive performance, alright, but its terseness will probably force most readers to follow up with pen and paper on the side to verify his arguments in detail. In this article we will mostly follow Zagier’s line, but with some slight expansions and glosses so that pen and paper is (hopefully) unnecessary.
          %h2#chebyshevs_estimate Chebyshev’s estimate
          %p In this section we prove a result essentially due to Chebyshev which puts us already in the neighborhood of the prime number theorem. Introduce a function
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi θ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi x
                  %mi log
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta(x) = \sum_{p \leq x} \log(p).
          %p
            By the following result, our job is done once we establish the relation
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi θ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta(x) \sim x
          #asymp.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              The asymptotic statement
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi θ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta(x) \sim x
              implies the prime number theorem.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p In one direction, we have an obvious inequality
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi θ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi x
                    %mi log
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≤
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi x
                    %mi log
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi log
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta(x) = \sum_{p \leq x} \log(p) \leq \sum_{p \leq x} \log(x) = \pi(x)\log(x).
            %p
              Assuming that
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi θ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∼
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta(x) \sim x
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
